The CSIPHY0 clock has to be on for the other three PHYs

In the SM7150 vendor device tree (sdmmagpie-camera.dtsi) the node of EVERY
CSIPHY asks for the CSIPHY0 clock, on top of its own. Verbatim, from the CSIPHY1
node -not the 0 one-:

    clocks = <&clock_camcc CAM_CC_CPHY_RX_CLK_SRC>,
        <&clock_camcc CAM_CC_CSIPHY0_CLK>,          <-- this one
        <&clock_camcc CAM_CC_CSIPHY1_CLK>,
        <&clock_camcc CAM_CC_CSI1PHYTIMER_CLK_SRC>,
        <&clock_camcc CAM_CC_CSI1PHYTIMER_CLK>;

Mainline does not ask for it, so when CSIPHY1 is used the clock of the 0 stays
off. Measured on the phone with clk_summary and the camera streaming:

    camcc_csiphy1_clk   en=1   rate=19200000
    camcc_csiphy0_clk   en=0   rate=19200000

And there is a second bug in the same table: the four "csiphyN" had their rate
at { 0 }, that is, no frequency was asked of them and they inherited the one of
their shared source cphy_rx_clk_src. That is why csiphy1 measured 19,2 MHz, the
crystal. The vendor runs these clocks at 384 MHz (svs) and 400 MHz (turbo):

    clock-rates = <384000000 0 0 300000000 0>,
                  <400000000 0 0 300000000 0>,
                  <400000000 0 0 300000000 0>;

WHAT SYMPTOM IT EXPLAINS
------------------------
The PHY with its clock at 19,2 MHz sees the low-power signalling -which is slow
and rail to rail- but cannot deserialise high speed. Which is exactly what was
measured throughout the investigation:

    the five lanes leave rest on emit           <- LP, works
    the PHY interrupts at the frame rate        <- LP, works
    not a single sync is decoded                <- HS, does not work
    not a packet reaches the CSID, nor a bad
      CRC, nor a MISR signature, nor an error
      bit                                       <- there is nothing to forward

And it explains the dismissals that fitted nothing: sweeping the settle count
from 12 to 44 gave the PHY state identical byte for byte -and the command DID
reach the hardware, verified-, and the link rate, the lane count (2, 3 and 4),
the PHY selector and the polarity did not move a bit either. They all adjust
WHEN or HOW something is sampled that the PHY never gets to deserialise.

It also explains why the REAR camera, on CSIPHY0 and with a different sensor,
gave zero as well: its clock was at { 0 } too.

Patch 0081 raised cphy_rx_clk_src to 400 MHz and dragged csiphy1 along with it,
but by accident and not by request. This makes it explicit and also turns on the
CSIPHY0 clock, which was still off.

Found by comparing the vendor device tree with the resource table, after
exhausting the CSID and CSIPHY register spaces.
